<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Entitlements for the *embedded interpreter* of the self-contained bundle —
	Contents/Resources/engine/.venv/bin/python3.12, the process the launcher's
	shim execs and the one that actually runs the ML stack. Applied by
	scripts/build-macos.sh together with codesign's `options runtime`; the
	vendored `sox` binary in Contents/Resources/engine/tools/bin gets the same
	file, because it is the only other Mach-O in the bundle that is an
	executable rather than a library.

	This is the second entitlements file that packaging/entitlements-app.plist's
	closing comment promised. Everything here is a permission the *interpreter*
	needs and the Rust binary does not; nothing here belongs in that file, and
	the app binary must not gain any of it to save a plist.

	Mind the prose, exactly as in entitlements-app.plist: a double hyphen is
	illegal inside an XML comment, and while plutil lints such a file happily,
	the parser codesign hands it to (AMFIUnserializeXML) rejects it outright
	with a bare "syntax error near line N". Write flag names without their
	leading dashes.

	NOT sandboxed, for the same reasons the app is not: the engine reads and
	writes audio anywhere the user points it, spawns isolated worker venvs, and
	talks to Core Audio directly.

	Plain .so / .dylib files — the several hundred that pip wheels drop into
	site-packages, plus torch's own lib dir — get *no* entitlements. An
	entitlement is a property of a main executable's code signature; a library
	loaded into someone else's process inherits that process's entitlements and
	its own would be ignored. They are signed (with the same identity, so the
	seal holds) and nothing more.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		The one that makes an embedded CPython work at all.

		The hardened runtime refuses to let a process dlopen any Mach-O whose
		signature does not chain to the same Team ID as the process's own (or
		to Apple). Every .so in every pip wheel is signed by its wheel builder,
		by the linker's ad hoc signature, or by nobody — none of which is us.
		Without this flag the first `import numpy` dies in dyld with
		"code signature invalid", and there is no way to hear about it earlier:
		the check happens at load, not at launch.

		Re-signing the whole wheel tree with our identity (which build-macos.sh
		does anyway, for the resource seal) does not remove the need. Library
		validation compares Team IDs, and a self-signed certificate has no Team
		ID at all, so even a uniformly re-signed tree fails the comparison. A
		real Developer ID build would have one and could in principle drop this
		flag, but only if every last .so were re-signed with it, which is
		exactly what we do and still cannot verify from here. Keep the flag.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
		llvmlite / numba. librosa (pulled in by kokoro and by the voice
		conversion paths) JIT compiles hot loops through numba, which allocates
		its code pages through LLVM's MCJIT memory manager. That manager asks
		for writable memory and then makes it executable, and it does so
		WITHOUT the MAP_JIT flag that com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit is scoped
		to. Under the hardened runtime that transition is refused and the
		process takes SIGKILL (Namespace CODESIGNING) at first compile, not at
		import, so it surfaces as "the engine died halfway through a
		conversion" rather than as a load error.

		allow-jit would be the tighter flag and is deliberately NOT used: it
		only covers MAP_JIT mappings, which is not what MCJIT makes. This is
		the entitlement Apple documents for exactly this case (unsigned
		executable memory from a JIT that predates MAP_JIT).
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
		The microphone and the system audio tap, again — and "again" is the
		point. The hardened runtime is per-process and does not propagate
		across posix_spawn, so the entitlement the app carries buys the engine
		nothing: the interpreter is a separate hardened process and needs its
		own pass through the same HAL gate, or every Core Audio *input* stream
		it opens is dead before TCC is ever consulted.

		TCC responsibility does flow the other way. The engine is spawned by
		Syrinx.app, so tccd attributes its recording to the app's bundle
		identity and answers it with the app's grant, with the app's usage
		strings on the prompt. The engine has no Info.plist and needs none.
		Two different mechanisms, both required, only one of them inherited.

		Note the spelling: com.apple.security.device.microphone is the App
		Sandbox key and does nothing here. This is the one the runtime honours.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
		Considered and left out:

		* com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — the launcher
		  sets PATH and a handful of SYRINX_/PYTHON_ variables, none of which
		  are DYLD_*. The vendored sox closure is re-pointed with
		  install_name_tool at build time precisely so that no DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH
		  is needed at runtime; adding the flag would make that laziness
		  possible, which is the wrong direction.

		* com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit — see the MCJIT note above. It does
		  not cover what numba does, and holding both flags would be strictly
		  more permission for no behaviour.

		* com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection — the
		  nuclear option that turns off W^X wholesale.
		  allow-unsigned-executable-memory is enough for MCJIT; this is not
		  needed and would be a much larger hole.

		* com.apple.security.automation.apple-events, .files.user-selected.* and
		  the rest of the App Sandbox keys — meaningless outside a sandbox.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
